{
  "origin": "A product question met at implementation used to have nowhere to go: the block gate offered retry, skip and stop, and an answer rode the retry comment into the re-dispatch and landed in no document — the code got built to a decision nobody recorded. The gate is now the fork itself: the task's header, the block in the register — what it is blocked on, what the executor found, the options, the recommendation — and then the sides as numbered rows with the recommended one first and Comment as the way to push back. Nothing else: no retry, no skip, no stop, because none of those puts an answer into the record. The pick then takes the second tier — landed in the owning discussion, the specification brought into line by corrigendum from `implementation/{topic}`, and only then onto the task and the executor continued. This walks that end to end and pins the order of it — the gate before the discussion write, the discussion before the corrigendum, the corrigendum before the executor runs again — so an answer can never reach the code ahead of the record.",
